    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 061059\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n359 AM MST Tue Oct 6 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 12z Aviation Discussion...\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Above normal temperatures with minimal day to day changes will\n  carry through the rest of this week as lower desert highs top \n  out around 98 to 107 degrees.\n\n- An Extreme Heat Watch is in effect for portions of southeast\n  California and southwest Arizona from Wednesday through Friday \n  as temperatures warm to near record levels, resulting in Major \n  HeatRisk.\n\n- Dry weather with zero rain chances will persist through Saturday\n  before a potential cooler and more unsettled weather pattern \n  arrives Sunday into early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nLatest GOES mid-lvl wv imagery and 500 mb objective analysis \nreveals a broad area of high pressure encompassing much of the \nDesert Southwest while a weak shortwave trough is progressing \nthrough the Intermountain West. Looking farther southwest, \nHurricane Rachel in the E Pacific is churning well west of the tip\nof the Baja California and continues to slowly progress wwd into \nopen waters. Moisture from Rachel will likely come into play \nlater this upcoming weekend, providing the next decent chance for \nrain across AZ and southeast CA. Until then, dry conditions are \nexpected through the remainder of this week. \n\nWe will continue a warming trend today and tomorrow as high \npressure builds over SoCal with positive hght anomalies expanding\nover the entire forecast area. The aforementioned weak shortwave \ntrough will slide through the Four Corners area over the next \ncouple days, bringing isolated showers to the high terrain of E \nAZ, but south-central AZ will remain dry. The most pronounced \npositive 500 mb hght anomalies today will again be focused along \nand west of the Lower Colorado River Valley where highs are \nexpected to top out around 99-104 degrees, which is a solid 8-10 \ndegrees above normal for early October. Highs in the central AZ \ndeserts will not be as hot as areas farther west, but are still \nexpected to reach the upper 90s to around 100 degrees in many \nlocations this afternoon. As mid-lvl hghts/thickness increase \nfurther on Wednesday, sfc temperatures will warm 2-4 degrees \nacross the board, with nearly all lower desert communities \nreaching 100+ degrees in the afternoon. The hottest locations \nwill again be along the Gila and Colorado River valleys as well \nas Imperial Valley where highs on Wednesday afternoon will reach \nthe 102-105 degree range, which is upwards of 10-12 degrees above \nnormal or near daily records. An Extreme Heat Watch remains in \neffect beginning on Wednesday for the Imperial Valley and Yuma.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nRidging aloft will reach peak strength over the western half of \nthe forecast area on Thursday with low-lvl thermal profiles \nresponding as 850 mb temperatures climb to around 26-27C. This \nwill result in sfc highs reaching 102-107 degrees across the \nwestern deserts, again nearing daily records in Yuma and the \nImperial Valley. Major HeatRisk is expected to be most expansive \non Thursday afternoon and therefore the Extreme Heat Watch has \nbeen expanded to include the entire Lower Colorado River valley \nand a portion of the Gila River valley in Yuma County including \nDateland and Tacna. Portions of south-central AZ will also see \nhighs climb into the low 100s or around 10 degrees above normal on\nThursday, although the HeatRisk is expected to remain in the \nModerate category. Friday will be a transition day where a \ndeepening trough over the Pac NW which in turn induces SW flow \naloft over the West Coast. Although we will begin to see a \nreduction in overall hghts aloft on Friday afternoon, low-lvl \nthermal profiles will be slow to respond as 850 mb temperatures \nare still expected to reach 25-26C across the much of the forecast\narea equating to sfc highs around 100-105 degrees in most lower \ndesert communities. Since the latest NBM forecast shows a 50-70% \nprobability of high temperatures reaching the Major HeatRisk \ncategory for the Imperial Valley and Yuma area, the decision was \nmade to extend the Extreme Heat Watch for those areas through \nFriday evening. \n\nWe then turn our attention to an upcoming pattern shift heading \ninto this weekend as most deterministic models and ensemble \nmembers are trending toward an overall troffing pattern setting up\nacross the western CONUS. There is now better model agreement \nthat a stronger and deeper trough will transition into a cutoff \nlow over the Pac NW on Saturday and gradually sink southward into \nCA Sunday and Monday. Meanwhile, latest forecast guidance from the\nNHC shows an eventual turn to the northeast by Rachel this \nweekend with the remnants of the storm itself being pulled \nnortheastward and phasing with the west coast trough. As this \npattern transpires, there should be an increase in southwesterly \nflow aloft over the Desert Southwest which will begin to pull \nPacific moisture into the region by late Saturday into Sunday. \nCoincidentally, rain chances will return to the much of the region\nwith NBM PoPs now around 20-50% across south-central AZ on Sunday\nand even up to 40-60% across the foothills NE of Phoenix by \nMonday. There is still high uncertainty in rainfall amounts at \nthis time but there is at least a chance of some higher totals \nexceeding 1.50\" across AZ high terrain. As 500 mb hghts begin to \nlower ahead of the approaching trough along with increasing cloud \ncover and moisture, high temperatures will take a dive from the \nmid 90s on Saturday to the low to mid 80s by Monday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1055Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo aviation weather concerns under a FEW occasional mid-level\ncloud decks can be expected throughout the TAF period. The overall\nwind pattern will return to a more traditional diurnal pattern\nwith the westerly shift likely to occur during the mid/late\nafternoon hours with overall speeds aob 7 kts. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo aviation weather concerns under clear skies can be expected\nthroughout the TAF period. Winds will generally be light and\nvariable with periods of calm conditions. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions with well above normal temperatures will continue \nthrough the week, with afternoon highs reaching up to 100-107 \ndegrees across the lower deserts (peaking Thursday). Winds will \nreturn to their more typical light diurnal tendencies today and\npersist into Thursday before increasing again Friday into this\nweekend. MinRHs will mostly range from 15-25% across the western \ndistricts to 20-35% across eastern districts through the week with\nfair to good overnight recovery (35-60%) area-wide. Humidities \nwill be lowest on Thursday when temperatures are likely at their \nhottest, but winds will still be light at this time, so there are \nno elevated fire weather concerns. No wetting rain expected \nthrough at least Saturday. However, chances for wetting rainfall\nis expected to arrive by the end of the weekend/start of next \nweek as a weather system begins to move into the region.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Watch from Wednesday morning through Friday evening \n     for AZZ532.\n\n     Extreme Heat Watch from Thursday morning through Thursday \n     evening for AZZ530-536.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Watch from Wednesday morning through Friday evening \n     for CAZ566-567.\n\n     Extreme Heat Watch from Thursday morning through Thursday \n     evening for CAZ569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Salerno\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...Lojero\nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict/Salerno\n",
